# `try { Below.prototype = made; risky(); kept = null; } catch (error) { alias = kept; }`: the store under `prototype` is the first operation of its block, and the block hands `made` to its handler.
# The arguments of a handler are read at the place of that first operation, so the object seemed to leave at the store alone: it passed for made to be the prototype and known under no other name.
# What was then stored through the alias reached no layout: `alias.hello = ..; new Below().hello()` entered the method of the base.
# f1 = Base, f2 = Below, f3 = risky
